  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nIssued 205 PM MDT WED OCT 28 2026\nSaturday, a closed low with an elongated southern trough begins\nto swing up and north of the forecast area, bringing the \ntrough, a cold front, and wetter conditions. This trough will \nhave a good pull of Pacific moisture ahead of it, with \nprecipitable water values increasing to around the 90th \npercentile in southwest Idaho. The anomalous atmospheric \nmoisture will be coincident with largescale forcing, leading to \nincreasing precipitation chances through the day Saturday, \nreaching 50-80% Saturday evening and night as a cold front \ncrosses the area. Hopefully your plan is to dress up as Mary \nPoppins for Halloween, just in case. Snow levels will initially \nbegin at 6-8 kft MSL, lowering to 4.5-6.5 kft MSL by Sunday \nafternoon. Lowering snow levels occur alongside decreasing \nprecipitation chances as conditions dry out behind the front. \nOverall, the bulk of any snow accumulation will remain above 6.5\nkft MSL, with generally 2-5 inches of accumulation (Locally up \nto 7 on the highest peaks). The lowering snow levels could allow\nfor snow or wintry mix as low as 4.5 kft Sunday, although \nlittle accumulation is anticipated as precipitation tapers off \nbehind the departing system. Ridging will build in behind the \ndeparting system, allowing for dry conditions Monday afternoon \nthrough at least mid-week. Temperatures will be seasonable or \nbelow normal throughout the period.",
